.TH VTPM-MDS-RECONCILE 8 "October 2026" "vtpm-mds" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
vtpm-mds-reconcile \- keep SPIRE node aliases in line with Proxmox pools and tags
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vtpm-mds-reconcile
.RB [ \-config
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-once ]
.RB [ \-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A guest enrolled by
.B vtpm-mds
holds an LDevID whose subject is
.BI CN= <vmid>
and which chains to its cluster's intermediate.
SPIRE's
.B tpm_devid
attestor turns that into the selectors
.BI tpm_devid:subject:cn: <vmid>
and
.BI tpm_devid:ca:fingerprint: <sha1>
for the intermediate.
Those two prove which VM of which cluster an agent is.
.PP
.B vtpm-mds-reconcile
runs once, beside the SPIRE server, not on a Proxmox node.
It reads each configured cluster's guests, pools and tags over the Proxmox API
and writes SPIRE node aliases that select exactly those two selectors.
The SPIFFE IDs of the aliases carry the VM's pool and tags, so that
registrations can name a role instead of a list of VM IDs, and so that a
removed tag or a destroyed VM takes its rights away without anything changing
in the guest or in its certificate.
.PP
That a VM is in a pool or carries a tag is the reconciler's statement, read
from the Proxmox API and written through the SPIRE admin socket.
It is not signed, and a relying party cannot check it afterwards; trust it as
far as anyone who holds SPIRE admin rights.
.SH WHY A TAG NEEDS A POOL
.SS Who can set a tag
Proxmox lets anyone with
.B VM.Config.Options
on a guest set its tags, which is the guest's owner.
The datacenter options
.B user-tag-access
(default
.BR free )
and
.B registered-tags
can narrow which tags such a user may set; a user with
.B Sys.Modify
on
.B /
is never narrowed.
With the default, the owner of any VM can write
.B vtpm_mds.io.role.db-admin
on it.
.SS So a tag alone is worth only as much as the VM's owner
If a tag alone granted a right, every VM owner could grant their VM every
right, including a sandbox VM created a minute ago.
A grant therefore has to name a scope that the VM's owner cannot choose by
themselves.
.SS Why the pool
A Proxmox pool is set per VM, and putting a VM into a pool, or taking it out,
needs
.B Pool.Allocate
on that pool; moving it between pools needs that right on both.
Pools are how Proxmox already delegates the administration of VMs.
So the pool is the scope, and a tag counts only together with the VM's pool:
the owner chooses the tag, an administrator of the pool chooses who may carry
it there.
.SS Which IDs a VM receives
For trust domain
.IR td ,
the configured cluster name
.I cluster
and a VM
.IR vmid :
.TP
.BI spiffe:// td /pve/ cluster /vm/ vmid
always.
.TP
.BI spiffe:// td /pve/ cluster /pool/ pool
when the VM is in a pool.
.TP
.BI spiffe:// td /pve/ cluster /pool/ pool /tag/ key / value
for each tag
.BI vtpm_mds.io. key . value
of a VM that is in a pool.
.PP
Pool, key and value are lowercased.
A tag with a character a SPIFFE path cannot hold (anything but
.BR a\-z ,
.BR 0\-9 ,
.BR . ,
.B \-
and
.BR _ ,
or a segment that is
.B .
or
.BR .. )
grants nothing and is logged.
Templates get no alias.
.SS What an owner does for a tag to take effect
Put the tag on the VM, and have a pool administrator put the VM into a pool.
Registrations then name the tag alias of that pool as their parent.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS A VM with a pool and tags
VM
.B 350
in pool
.B web
with the tags
.B vtpm_mds.io.role.Frontend
and
.B vtpm_mds.io.role.cache
receives
.BR \&.../vm/350 ,
.BR \&.../pool/web ,
.B \&.../pool/web/tag/role/frontend
and
.BR \&.../pool/web/tag/role/cache .
A workload entry whose parent is
.B \&.../pool/web/tag/role/frontend
runs on every VM of pool
.B web
that carries that tag, and on no other.
.SS A VM without a pool
VM
.B 351
in no pool with the tag
.B vtpm_mds.io.role.admin
receives only
.BR \&.../vm/351 .
The tag grants nothing.
Adding the VM to a pool makes it effective within one interval.
.SS A tag that cannot be a path segment
VM
.B 352
in pool
.B web
with the tag
.B vtpm_mds.io.role.a+b
receives
.B \&.../vm/352
and
.BR \&.../pool/web ;
the tag is logged once per pass and grants nothing.
.SS A removed tag
Removing
.B vtpm_mds.io.role.cache
from VM
.B 350
deletes
.B \&.../pool/web/tag/role/cache
for that VM on the next pass.
A workload holding an SVID under it keeps it until the SVID expires.
.SS A destroyed VM
When VM
.B 353
is missing from two consecutive good reads of its cluster, its aliases are
deleted and every
.B tpm_devid
agent naming
.B cn:353
with that cluster's intermediate is banned.
A ban cuts the agent off at its next call to the server.
A VM restored from a backup after that must enroll again
.RB ( devid\-enroll ),
because its old LDevID is banned.
.SS A nested pool
VM
.B 354
in pool
.B web/front
receives only
.BR \&.../vm/354 ;
the pool is logged.
Nested pools are not served.
.SS Two pools that differ only in case
Pools
.B Web
and
.B web
of one cluster would both become
.BR pool/web .
Neither yields a pool or tag alias, and both are logged, so that one pool can
never receive the other's grants.
.SH SAFETY
.TP
.B Only its own prefix
The reconciler creates and deletes only entries whose parent is
.BI spiffe:// td /spire/server
and whose SPIFFE ID starts with
.BI spiffe:// td /pve/ cluster /
for a configured cluster.
A node alias written by hand under that prefix is deleted.
Workload entries, whatever their SPIFFE ID, are never touched.
.TP
.B Doubtful reads
A cluster whose API cannot be read, whose listing holds no guest at all, or
whose token lacks
.B VM.Audit
on
.B /vms
or
.B Pool.Audit
on
.B /pool
with propagation, is left unchanged for that pass and does not count toward a
ban.
Proxmox hides a guest the token may not see instead of refusing the request:
a
.B NoAccess
ACL on one guest makes that guest look destroyed.
.TP
.B Bans
Only after two consecutive good reads, and never with
.BR \-once .
The count is held in memory; a restart starts it again.
.TP
.B Renaming a cluster
in the configuration leaves the aliases under the old prefix, which no
longer belongs to the reconciler; delete them by hand.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-config " path"
The configuration file; default
.IR /etc/vtpm\-mds\-reconcile/config.yaml .
.TP
.B \-once
Run one pass and exit, without banning: 0 on success, 1 on failure.
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
YAML, decoded strictly: an unknown key stops the start.
.PP
.nf
trust_domain: example.org
spire_socket: /run/spire/server/private/api.sock
interval: 60s
clusters:
  \- name: cluster\-1
    api_url: https://pve\-a.example:8006
    ca_file: /etc/vtpm\-mds\-reconcile/cluster\-1\-ca.pem
    token_file: /run/credentials/vtpm\-mds\-reconcile.service/cluster\-1.token
    intermediates:
      \- /etc/vtpm\-mds\-reconcile/cluster\-1\-intermediate.pem
.fi
.PP
.B interval
defaults to 60 s and must be at least 10 s.
A cluster name must be a SPIFFE path segment and unique.
Every cluster needs at least one intermediate; with two (a rotation) every
alias exists once per intermediate.
A self-signed certificate is refused as an intermediate, and so is one
configured for two clusters, because
.B ca:fingerprint
covers every certificate of the chain and a shared one would match the other
cluster's agents.
The token file holds
.IB user @ realm ! tokenid = secret ;
the token needs
.B PVEAuditor
on
.BR / ,
for the user and the token.
At start a token without
.B VM.Audit
on
.B /vms
or
.B Pool.Audit
on
.B /pool
stops the process, naming the cluster and the privilege.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vtpm\-mds (8),
.BR devid\-enroll (1),
.BR spire\-server (1)
